{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789493220,"nonce":"dVZSeJgvPQflCyUCqk1ugvgJr+w=","message":"Phase 2 landed: 5dd5012 (vector instructions for the primitives), 482186d\n(VecOp with typed operands, v128_copy), ab8036e (slot promotion),\nfc2e46c (benchmarks).\n\nShape, as built:\n\n- A call to a `simd` native lowers to `Instr::VecOp { v, fun, dst, out,\n  args }` whose operands are `VecArg::{Slot, Array, Scalar, Value}` and\n  whose result is `VecOut::{Slot, Array, Scalar, Value}`. With slots and\n  arrays only it is the memory-to-memory native; `serialize` writes the\n  call back and the walker calls the native, so the interpreter never\n  changes. Effect and alias classes follow the operands (RawBytes,\n  ArrayData; pure when everything is a value), so GVN/LICM order it\n  against the slot's other accesses.\n- Cranelift and LLVM emit the lane operation between unaligned loads of\n  the slot operands and a store to the destination. A vector VALUE is\n  `\u003c4 x i32\u003e` / `I32X4` (`ValueData { ty: Int, lanes: 4 }`), bitcast to\n  the operation's lanes; that keeps every existing lanes\u003e1 mechanism\n  (backend types, walker lane table, serializer lane registers) intact.\n- `sroa` gains `Shape::Slot`: `alloc_bytes(16)` whose pointer is only a\n  whole-slot operand (offset 0) of a VecOp, or a `MemGet`/`MemSet` at a\n  constant lane offset of 4- or 8-byte width, becomes a vector value;\n  phis come from the existing web machinery (the loop-carried `acc = acc\n  + x` case is exactly its `z = phi(initial, per-iteration)` web).\n  Lane accesses become `VecExtract`/`VecInsert`; a fresh slot is a\n  `VecSplat` of an `Int 0` the pass defines at the entry block. Anything\n  else -- a field store, a call argument, a return, a non-constant lane\n  offset, byte or short lanes -- escapes and the web stays memory, which\n  is the materialisation point the design called for.\n- `serialize` gives values slots back: `Function::simd_scratch` records\n  `alloc_bytes` and the Bytes/Int types, the prologue allocates one\n  scratch per operand position of the widest VecOp, each value spills its\n  four lane registers with SetMem before the call and reads back with\n  GetMem after. Slow, but only the flat interpreter runs it, and\n  `--emit-optimized` stays runnable on stock hl.\n- The walker runs a VecOp on values through 16-byte buffers and the\n  native; lane ops through the same typed read/write the memory\n  instruction did.\n\nResult on the value-form dot product (examples/simd/Main.hx `values`):\nLLVM IR is `load, load, fmul, fadd` with the accumulator a `\u003c4 x float\u003e`\nphi and no allocation; x86-64 AOT is vmovups/vmulps/vaddps. NUC, 4 cores,\nstock hl 1.14 + simd.hdll vs ash: simd_dot 2.51s vs 0.05s (jit) / 0.11s\n(hybrid); simd_particles 2.18s vs 0.11s / 0.12s. Every engine prints the\nsame checksum as stock hl.\n\nRefusals worth a later pass: a promoted value stored into an object\nfield costs an allocation per store (particles: two per step); the\nabstract's methods as standalone functions return the pointer (only the\ninlined copies promote); `Int8x16`/`Int16x8` lane access has no scratch\nserialization so those webs would refuse (no value types for them yet).\n\nNot done from the plan: interception keyed on `Class.method` names. It\nturned out unnecessary: the inliner exposes the natives inside the\nabstract's methods and slot promotion removes the allocations, so the\nsource-agnostic route reaches the same code without a name table. wasm\nSIMD128 and 256-bit lanes remain.","files":null}]}